## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The fact-check confirms that Russia did send a shipment of medical supplies, including ventilators, masks, and respirators, to the United States in early April 2020, following a phone call between Presidents Trump and Putin. The shipment was acknowledged and accepted by U.S. officials, with President Trump publicly thanking Russia. However, the nature of the transaction was disputed: Russia characterized it as humanitarian aid, while the U.S. stated it was a purchase, and later reports indicated costs were split. Furthermore, the Russian-made ventilators from the shipment were subsequently returned to Russia due to safety concerns after they were linked to fatal hospital fires.

### Step 2: Missing Information
The claim is specifically dated October 21, 2020, but the evidence only documents a shipment in early April 2020. The fact-check contains no information about any separate Russian offer or shipment of medical aid to the United States in October 2020. Therefore, we lack evidence to verify if the claim refers to the well-documented April 2020 event or to an entirely different, unreported October 2020 action.

### Step 3: Conclusion
The core claim that Russia sent medical equipment to the U.S. to help with coronavirus is supported by evidence, but this occurred in April 2020, not October 2020 as stated. The fact-check provides no information to corroborate an event in October 2020. Since the claim's specific date is a crucial part of the assertion and is not supported by the available evidence, the correct decision is `not enough information`.

### Verdict: NEI

### Justification
Evidence confirms Russia shipped medical supplies—including [ventilators, masks, and respirators](https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/05/21/860143691/u-s-sends-ventilators-to-russia-in-5-6-million-coronavirus-aid-package)—to the United States in [early April 2020](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/russia-welcomes-us-ventilators-coronavirus-good-will-usaid-banned-by-moscow/), an action [acknowledged by U.S. officials](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/russian-plane-coronavirus-medical-gear-lands-u-s-after-trump-n1174436), though the terms (gift vs. purchase) were [disputed](https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/05/21/860143691/u-s-sends-ventilators-to-russia-in-5-6-million-coronavirus-aid-package). However, the claim is dated October 21, 2020, and the fact-check uncovered no information about any separate aid shipment in that month; therefore, the specific timeframe of the claim remains unverified.